#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180508} :activity/id #uuid "564a18a1-f71e-5757-9e58-d7176c63906f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/id #uuid "04ff54ad-5d9d-5751-8a07-6602f0f05410" :topic/math-academy-id 3717} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180726 :tutorial/content "Let $\\psi(x,y)$ be a two-variable function defined on a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ We know that for a point $(x_0,y_0)\\in D,$ the vector\n\n$$\n\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}\n$$\n\nis *tangential* to the level curve $\\psi(x,y) = \\psi(x_0,y_0).$ \n\nBy computing this tangent vector at *every* point, we can generate a vector *field* tangential to the level curves of $\\psi$ at every point.\n\nWith that in mind, let \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nbe a vector field defined on $D.$ A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nTo construct a vector field with this stream function, we first compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j=2y\\,i-2x\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA diagram showing the surface $z = \\psi(x,y),$ its level curves, and the vector field $\\mathbf F$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/69/696e6c623dbc3ecfae2643a7317d3d176294624289ecf6efb16debdc947d0394.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ are circles of the form $x^2+y^2=c$, and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ in the $xy$-plane is tangential to these level curves.\n\n- Suppose $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity of a fluid in the $xy$-plane. Then, the level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ represent the path of a massless particle in the $xy$-plane moving with the flow. For this reason, the level curves of $\\psi$ are called **streamlines.**\n\n- The stream function of a vector field is typically not unique since we can add any constant to $\\psi$ to generate another valid stream function." :tutorial/id #uuid "9feba9cd-6bee-5c74-a1ab-940f686f38e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5856 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8704325f-a4d0-5908-8630-87a2fdd6ce55" :step/math-academy-id 35867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179426 :question/id #uuid "a779f67a-21ee-5eb4-8355-c69476ee0d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12395"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7abceeb-620d-5e95-93ca-30bc557cf007" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490304}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Vector Field From Its Stream Function"} :step/id #uuid "f95a8abc-f190-54e3-ab9c-c5ca8d31c613" :step/math-academy-id 44491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179427 :question/id #uuid "69a293cc-ff8c-5485-8329-12e87e1addff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12396"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc613be0-2d34-5253-8fbe-7d2a5fcd0840" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050189}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499535}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cases Where a Stream Function Exists"} :step/id #uuid "0aa49923-0dc0-553a-a533-d846d4f56429" :step/math-academy-id 44492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179435 :question/id #uuid "6cb53e89-c4a9-57a6-b54f-f6a09d3a17d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12439"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30cae81-5bda-52dc-8de4-9ad5fd587e48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499564}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Streamlines of Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "dcb816eb-91e7-58ce-82b1-214fe5d095a2" :step/math-academy-id 44698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180759 :tutorial/content "Let's find a stream function for the following vector field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + e^x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nNote that our vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ has the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad\nQ(x,y) = e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nLet's start by checking that a stream function exists. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function since $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nTo find a stream function, we **partially integrate** these two equations and compare the results:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $y$ (that is, treating $x$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+f(x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is an arbitrary function.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $x$ (that is, treating $y$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+g(y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is an arbitrary function.\n\nComparing our two expressions for $\\psi,$ we get equality by setting $f=g=0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = -e^x \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFinally, remember that the streamlines of $\\mathbf F$ are given by the level curves of $\\psi.$ So, to find the streamlines of $\\mathbf F,$ we plot the curves $\\psi = k$ for different values of $k.$ The level curves of $\\psi$ are always tangential to $\\mathbf F.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc3ea425-1287-5a1d-ad21-3528a9c3d57e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7704 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Stream Function From Its Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "173559da-bde2-5974-ae89-c0d7f7949615" :step/math-academy-id 44700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179437 :question/id #uuid "6cf11fc9-874f-5d59-bff0-1ed3debcbd40" :question/math-academy-id "e-12446"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffa79034-bdcc-5524-aa39-e81a79b48ded" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499504}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Stream Function for a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "f7a796a3-baa3-5a37-a25b-6048cc6cce34" :step/math-academy-id 44750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180763 :tutorial/content "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist. \n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true. \n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61b21c71-3509-5455-86ba-1898b599345d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7744 :tutorial/title "An Existence Condition for Stream Functions"} :step/id #uuid "fb48f733-4224-542a-8186-b1bda7425749" :step/math-academy-id 44836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180552}}] :activity/title "Stream Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179868}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499616} :activity/id #uuid "3e0c32a3-e400-5e80-92e0-610abde1479e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/id #uuid "04ff54ad-5d9d-5751-8a07-6602f0f05410" :topic/math-academy-id 3717} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d194298-7b9f-5375-a63e-abe0fd00183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498909} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b43ea-6957-5564-abd5-fa3a9b69baee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499007} {:answer/id #uuid "83dea68e-c1c5-56fd-83b2-641ed6509139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499027} {:answer/id #uuid "d1084bbd-c9b1-5b9e-aba8-ccbe75dad070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499193} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d04b97-b925-5d6c-8da1-814296c0c804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499202} :answer-field/id #uuid "000a0f4a-ae7b-5f51-a759-311c42f36adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "310694c7-b1be-5cf8-b20f-09c0a8e64636" :question/math-academy-id "q-162638" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+4y)\\,i-(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4{x}^{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x-(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "374bcc7d-ec2b-5e61-9b8f-f3e9cc285dcd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2467c5-2c2d-5b5d-bc2b-28eb5cd0d0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/ef27d204f39550af6a529640dd3e3c24f13dcd7c7d9631c5fd6d2a6d1e47ebcf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498906} {:answer/id #uuid "56d8e193-cdfa-5f8f-af06-4ddefe6b4ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/91/91e7124ef57462e9d8e0045582ccc8149608c687f3a822071345fa5b5f532cdd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498928} {:answer/id #uuid "66c14112-d0b5-53d0-8392-f270d6eec5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/42/42e1190d821286b8f66f39850d6c895b2801ef3e3f736d4e4df80df2e6ce513d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498961} {:answer/id #uuid "8332446a-894a-5efa-b549-105c9d96f0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d0/d060084a75147a69ffd6a74da31bfad2f9ad807dedddb5ee3b8fc20993432f9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499025} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc9ec84-1b7b-5e9c-a576-3127b03fef54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2ad340e6cd5a65e7f563ac898e7a56805a6d61e53ece75a6a4469031c60a62a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499220} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c353f18-26f0-57c7-b34f-03eb3c1ed93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "faed1d58-d701-5e29-bb64-3a9beae53a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162567" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f2f77c6c2047dcd3f542fc0f640a68b45a4d40784d0e7eaacdcadb002c79de5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nOf the given options, the only level curves tangential to the vector field at every point are shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd47775ffb4cba4cc01dde0bf404dca12bdeecc24e2f58639c04f1feda8293ee.png)"} :step/id #uuid "65923646-0a7d-5ade-9535-52fba8e7f6b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eccd188-ea83-597f-abf8-03ad9133061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b83a05a3fd79bf42ef45a16565194bf104214647a06144641e9b7bd66613ffaf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498836} {:answer/id #uuid "57f76c7f-ba08-5d98-870b-229dd3bdfcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4ad2cbbaccce4edae6e7e4901c6269a91aae1b364beba9b8bcc419d8e32c0e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498932} {:answer/id #uuid "675416c8-3285-5ede-b099-55462b554c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a2/a2fd8b46cf90aa751bfbd5f6c39257b8097a4898a5f5807bf5876a462d925fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498963} {:answer/id #uuid "9eedb55c-c4b8-5b99-a700-dafb5e8a0479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2a4e9e152f2da6d5b254cc6f3eb6f6c6d71dcf96e139116753587f7663d39b2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499067} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d994a1-a196-5c00-a356-dbb042053b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/97/9708321a993fcee685037db3ff120203d00a0c62892cf3712139e9afaa0426fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498836} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb9fdb9-29b3-51cf-8b8d-3e71313f6843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e84ccf0-2025-55a1-91ce-ccafeeadc53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162621" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=x-1$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=0$ or $g(y)=-y,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=xy-y$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-y & =c \\\\ y(x-1) & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=1$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dcf779041bbae1ce0a6c89042277b4dc33bb1eabe16d42a7824945a118951e0b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "794a8d11-192c-55a5-a0c6-593db9195676" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2837348b-6d6d-5f9c-b59a-17f08f8c704b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498816} {:answer/id #uuid "2f63601b-8c18-5c62-9c1d-b23197c0248e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{sin}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498839} {:answer/id #uuid "61678505-c4a2-51bf-9209-a1b476663d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498952} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7cb5ce-c76c-525b-952b-985fcb08f78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499060} {:answer/id #uuid "e371cf2c-6265-557b-893f-ac88cb328896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499241} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a53c735-9b72-59d0-8d36-96505245d570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8662a311-148d-52aa-9581-67da3a804b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-162537" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b9e6b2cd-32af-5db6-aad9-b1a728102b9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d03c985-35da-5986-ad82-1850226331bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498827} {:answer/id #uuid "2d390a46-6a6d-580f-aedb-aa1ff6dfc987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498830} {:answer/id #uuid "50ac5137-180c-55d6-9f11-2a2575b2db27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498919} {:answer/id #uuid "82a30966-fd2e-5ead-9908-43144c65211f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499023} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1e3bd-329c-592b-a22f-764ef4182668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498827} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb50c3b-df4c-5b33-bad4-52581805f032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7c0eb25-897e-5093-b34f-0455f3db55f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162615" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=({y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y)\\,i+({x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+4{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i-3y\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-6xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-6xy)+6xy=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =16{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+16{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-3)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e363175b-25ee-538d-8a8a-ba6522f9725a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499597}}] :activity/title "Stream Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498721}]}
